            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Extreme anguish, despair, remorse, and furious anger, typically representing the profound suffering of the wicked facing divine judgment or eternal separation from God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 8:12\",\"Matthew 13:42\",\"Matthew 13:50\",\"Matthew 22:13\",\"Matthew 24:51\",\"Matthew 25:30\",\"Luke 13:28\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Hostile rage, malice, and active rebellion against God or the righteous, emphasizing defiant anger rather than solely painful remorse.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 37:12\",\"Psalm 112:10\",\"Acts 7:54\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Temporal, historical judgment (such as the destruction of Jerusalem in AD 70) experienced by unfaithful Israel, rather than a description of the afterlife.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 24:51\",\"Luke 13:28\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The terminal despair of the wicked immediately preceding their complete destruction or cessation of existence (Annihilationism), rather than eternal conscious torment.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 13:42\",\"Matthew 13:50\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The phrase is predominantly found as an idiom in the Synoptic Gospels, especially Matthew, often paired with 'weeping' to comprehensively describe the sorrow (weeping) and bitter anger or regret (gnashing of teeth) of those excluded from the Kingdom.\"}",
